WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.229 --> 00:00:16.790
- Hi everyone, welcome to the
Daily Homeroom livestream.

00:00:16.790 --> 00:00:19.200
We're doing it a little
bit earlier than normal

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.440
'cause we have a guest that
we really wanted to talk to

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:25.130
who was available (chuckles)
earlier than normal.

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:26.430
First of all, for those of you wondering,

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:27.770
this new location,

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:30.680
they are cutting down trees
outside of my normal location,

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:32.240
and my kids are getting a little bit loud

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:33.313
downstairs as well.

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:35.770
Before we get into the conversation,

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:37.990
I will make the announcement
that I always make

00:00:37.990 --> 00:00:39.790
reminding folks that Khan Academy

00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:42.230
is a not-for-profit organization.

00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:44.840
We only exist because we have support

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:46.410
from folks like yourself.

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:49.190
Special call-out to several corporations

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:51.510
who have stepped up in
the last several months,

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:54.010
especially when they found out
we were running at a deficit,

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:57.810
and we were trying to step
up for the COVID response

00:00:57.810 --> 00:00:59.320
and our costs went up.

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:02.420
So special thanks to Bank
of America, Google.org,

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:05.500
Fastly, AT&amp;T, and Novartis.

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:08.920
So with that, I am excited to
jump into our conversation.

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:11.470
We have Bill Lewis today,

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:14.170
someone who I've known for some time

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:16.460
and have always enjoyed our conversations.

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:20.070
And I think there's a lot of
things going on in the world

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:22.600
that I would love to get
Bill's perspective on.

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:24.740
So Bill, great to have you.

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:26.890
- My pleasure, great
to be here, thank you.

00:01:28.523 --> 00:01:30.840
- So I guess just to introduce you,

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.110
you're the co-chairman of Lazard,

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:38.110
a very well-respected
investment bank on Wall Street.

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:42.020
And, you know, I think
given your background

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:44.090
there's many things we
can talk about today.

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:45.870
You know, there's the economy.

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:49.140
There's obviously what's
going on in America

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:52.240
around race relations and policing,

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:54.630
but maybe a good place to start is

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:57.600
tell us a little bit about your journey.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:59.170
Did you always think
that you would one day

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:01.868
become co-chair of Lazard?

00:02:01.868 --> 00:02:02.890
(Bill laughing)

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:03.843
- Yes, no.

00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:08.860
Look, I know we don't have a lot of time,

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:11.520
and in my estimation, you're right.

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:13.620
There are a lot of
things we can talk about.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:15.320
I guess I've got, is this good, yeah.

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:17.990
I guess there are a lot of
things that we can talk about.

00:02:17.990 --> 00:02:19.840
I hope we spend a fair amount of time

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:23.430
talking about race in
America because I think

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:28.080
we have not seen anything
like this since 1968.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:30.530
But that's probably a good
place for me to start.

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:34.030
I grew up in Richmond, Virginia,

00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:35.823
the capital of the confederacy.

00:02:38.025 --> 00:02:42.180
I went to segregated schools in Richmond

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:45.640
for the first eight years,
actually nine years,

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:48.700
kindergarten through the eighth grade.

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:52.570
As most people know, Brown v.
Board of Education was decided

00:02:52.570 --> 00:02:56.120
by the Supreme Court in 1954,

00:02:56.120 --> 00:03:01.120
and Richmond really didn't
comply with that until 1970,

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:02.663
so 16 years later.

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:07.700
In 1970, the journey that I'm on now

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:09.900
really took a different turn.

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:13.480
I received a full scholarship
through an organization

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:16.263
called A Better Chance, ABC,

00:03:18.045 --> 00:03:19.650
to go off to boarding school

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:22.040
and I went to Philips Academy

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:25.990
in Andover, Massachusetts for four years.

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:28.880
And after Andover I
went to Harvard College

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:33.410
majoring in economics, and
then I went to Wall Street

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:35.030
where I started at Morgan Stanley

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:37.220
in a two-year analyst program.

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:39.060
And then I went to
Harvard Business School,

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:40.560
and then I came back to Morgan Stanley.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.730
I spent 24 years at Morgan Stanley,

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:45.683
ultimately running their
global banking business.

00:03:46.698 --> 00:03:48.430
And then I joined Lazard,

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:51.670
where I've been for the last 16 years.

00:03:51.670 --> 00:03:54.310
So that's sort of one part of the journey.

00:03:54.310 --> 00:03:57.250
Along the way, I've been involved

00:03:57.250 --> 00:03:59.680
in lots of not-for-profits,

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:04.063
many around education.

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:09.310
I sat on the board of
Andover for 16 years.

00:04:09.310 --> 00:04:13.170
I now sit on the board of
Uncommon Charter Schools,

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:15.543
the largest charter in the Northeast.

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:18.450
I sit on the board of
the Posse Foundation.

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:20.030
I sit on the board of
the Research Alliance

00:04:20.030 --> 00:04:22.453
for New York City Public Schools.

00:04:25.015 --> 00:04:26.550
I'm about to go on the board

00:04:28.036 --> 00:04:31.830
of the Foundation for Child Development,

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:34.830
and most recently, I joined
the board of The City Fund,

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:37.360
an organization that has been founded

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:40.050
by Reed Hastings and John Arnold.

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:42.320
In addition, I sit on the board

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:44.420
of the New York City Police Foundation

00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:46.990
which is an interesting
contrast to the organization

00:04:46.990 --> 00:04:49.530
that I co-chaired some time ago

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:50.920
called the Legal Defense Fund,

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:52.120
which is actually an organization

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:54.450
that was started by Thurgood Marshall.

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:57.710
So I've sort of gone full
circle in that regard.

00:04:57.710 --> 00:05:00.900
And I sit on the board of the
New York Presbyterian Hospital

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:04.080
which has become the
largest hospital system

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:06.580
dealing with COVID in the United States.

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:10.000
So in a nutshell, that
has been my journey.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:11.850
That is how I'm spending
my time these days

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:14.803
in addition to talking to
CEOs on a regular basis.

00:05:19.130 --> 00:05:19.980
I can't hear you.

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:23.850
- Sorry, I had muted it

00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:26.070
'cause my five-year-old
was screaming downstairs

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:28.000
so I didn't wanna, but no I was saying,

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:30.910
no, I think that's really
helpful for people to appreciate

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:33.660
just the breadth of your
perspective that you've had

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:37.126
especially in some of these
organizations you're now on.

00:05:37.126 --> 00:05:39.500
Maybe getting straight
to the moment we're in,

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:41.210
you know, you mentioned that
we haven't seen something

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:44.000
like this since 1968.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:46.980
What do you think it is about now

00:05:49.406 --> 00:05:51.760
that is different maybe,

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:55.200
or that everything is
culminating to a head?

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:57.423
And what do you think
this moment will mean?

00:05:59.305 --> 00:06:02.350
- I think not to be flip,

00:06:02.350 --> 00:06:06.110
but we really have Steve Jobs to thank

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:10.133
in terms of why this moment
is different, the iPhone.

00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:14.840
We saw two terrible incidents,

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:17.110
two very different incidents

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:20.030
that were captured on videotape,

00:06:20.030 --> 00:06:22.040
but two very powerful incidents.

00:06:22.040 --> 00:06:24.610
Obviously, the callous

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:27.920
murder of George Floyd

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:32.300
by the police officer in
Minneapolis was different.

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:36.300
I don't think any of us, or
certainly not a lot of us

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:39.390
have ever seen someone killed

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:42.273
on live television.

00:06:43.670 --> 00:06:48.090
I don't care who you are, where you are,

00:06:48.090 --> 00:06:50.500
that was a moment in time.

00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:53.563
That was a modern-day lynching.

00:06:56.420 --> 00:07:00.620
And that has touched a
nerve unlike anything else

00:07:00.620 --> 00:07:02.690
that we've seen in this country.

00:07:02.690 --> 00:07:06.570
The second incident that
was captured on video

00:07:08.790 --> 00:07:11.350
was the scene at Central Park,

00:07:11.350 --> 00:07:14.303
which is literally right
across the street from me.

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:19.560
That in a lot of ways
was just as powerful,

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:22.523
not to downplay the
significance of a murder.

00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:28.490
But there was an African
American man who went to Harvard

00:07:28.700 --> 00:07:33.190
like I did, like my son does
now, my youngest son does now.

00:07:33.190 --> 00:07:36.040
That was a man who was
talking very peacefully

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:41.040
and very respectively to a
woman who was violating our laws

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:42.740
here in New York,

00:07:42.740 --> 00:07:43.940
and there was a woman

00:07:44.780 --> 00:07:47.990
who is known figuratively

00:07:47.990 --> 00:07:52.380
by virtually everybody in New York City,

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:53.920
everybody in the world of finance.

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:57.910
This was a 40-year-old
well-educated, highly educated woman

00:07:57.910 --> 00:08:02.690
who works for, who worked for
a major financial institution.

00:08:02.690 --> 00:08:07.670
So this was a person that
all of us metaphorically

00:08:07.670 --> 00:08:09.891
deal with every day.

00:08:09.891 --> 00:08:13.610
And she was very thoughtful;
it was all premeditated.

00:08:13.610 --> 00:08:17.860
She told this African American
man what she was going to do,

00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:19.490
how she was going to lie

00:08:21.451 --> 00:08:24.210
and weaponize race, if you will.

00:08:26.050 --> 00:08:29.450
Watching those two videos
made us all realize

00:08:29.450 --> 00:08:33.280
in very different ways that
we are in a moment in time

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:37.690
that we need to stop and
take stock of who we are

00:08:37.690 --> 00:08:40.530
and what we're trying to
accomplish as a country,

00:08:40.530 --> 00:08:43.890
and why not a lot has changed

00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:47.420
since 1968 when we last had

00:08:47.420 --> 00:08:51.913
very very violent social
unrest in our country.

00:08:54.310 --> 00:08:56.660
- And I think that is really possible.

00:08:56.660 --> 00:08:59.050
You know, we were all aware
of all of the different things

00:08:59.050 --> 00:09:00.730
that have been happening
over the last several weeks

00:09:00.730 --> 00:09:02.000
or months or frankly years,

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:04.140
but I think kind of the
bookend of those two issues

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:04.973
'cause I think that's right.

00:09:04.973 --> 00:09:08.050
That second one, it didn't
result in someone's death,

00:09:08.050 --> 00:09:12.440
but it very transparently
showed that people,

00:09:12.440 --> 00:09:15.990
people that we would expect
much better of frankly,

00:09:15.990 --> 00:09:18.370
that they were in many ways, I mean,

00:09:18.370 --> 00:09:20.710
just to add context to it, this is a woman

00:09:20.710 --> 00:09:22.220
who's threatening to call the police,

00:09:22.220 --> 00:09:26.380
and she knew that in kind
of this he said she said,

00:09:26.380 --> 00:09:28.040
she would get the benefit of the doubt.

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:31.520
And if she told the police
that he was threatening her

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:35.003
that it could be a scary thing for him.

00:09:36.410 --> 00:09:40.020
What do you think, you
know, to your point,

00:09:40.020 --> 00:09:42.190
having everyone having smartphones,

00:09:42.190 --> 00:09:43.500
it sheds some light on this,

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:45.520
but obviously for every
incident that is recorded,

00:09:45.520 --> 00:09:48.370
there's probably another
hundred that don't get recorded.

00:09:49.460 --> 00:09:51.020
How do you think this plays out?

00:09:51.020 --> 00:09:53.840
Like, so the first step
is to be able to recognize

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:56.853
it is happening, to have
documentation, and so we are there.

00:09:58.120 --> 00:09:59.470
Where do you think this goes?

00:09:59.470 --> 00:10:03.226
What are the reforms that
you think need to happen?

00:10:03.226 --> 00:10:05.100
And some of them could be legal reform.

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:07.720
Is it something in how
policemen are trained?

00:10:07.720 --> 00:10:10.260
But there's other things that
I guess you can only describe

00:10:10.260 --> 00:10:11.920
as cultural reform.

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:12.753
So,

00:10:14.783 --> 00:10:17.380
how do you think it plays
out or should play out?

00:10:17.380 --> 00:10:19.840
- So Sal, that's a great question,

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:22.240
and let me sort of ramble a bit.

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:25.890
First of all, let me
say that I don't think,

00:10:25.890 --> 00:10:28.660
and I could be naive and
I could be proven wrong,

00:10:28.660 --> 00:10:32.900
I don't think for every one incident

00:10:32.900 --> 00:10:37.280
that is captured on video there
are a hundred that are not

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:38.610
because that would suggest

00:10:40.590 --> 00:10:45.260
that we've only seen 1%
of the sort of violence

00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:48.550
and indifference that exists.

00:10:48.550 --> 00:10:51.190
And I don't think our country is that bad,

00:10:51.190 --> 00:10:52.860
but I take your point.

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:55.113
We are not, almost by definition,

00:10:55.980 --> 00:11:00.450
we can't possibly see and know
everything that is going on.

00:11:00.450 --> 00:11:02.990
And even as we saw in the case

00:11:03.980 --> 00:11:07.380
of the jogger in Georgia being murdered,

00:11:07.380 --> 00:11:09.540
even when there is video,

00:11:09.540 --> 00:11:13.433
there's no guarantee
that we're gonna see it.

00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:17.110
So look, I think

00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:22.103
in terms of framing where we go from here,

00:11:23.080 --> 00:11:25.260
I think there will be

00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:30.260
several versions of actions,

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:34.580
of a transformation of our society.

00:11:34.580 --> 00:11:36.570
And I'm not a big tech person.

00:11:36.570 --> 00:11:40.223
Witness how difficult it was
for me to dial into your call.

00:11:41.320 --> 00:11:44.980
But what we saw, and in some cases

00:11:45.990 --> 00:11:49.050
what we are seeing,

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:53.870
is what I call version 1.0 of a reckoning.

00:11:53.870 --> 00:11:57.810
And that is how leading institutions,

00:11:57.810 --> 00:12:02.343
whether they're colleges,
high schools, businesses,

00:12:03.460 --> 00:12:05.930
political offices, are all putting out

00:12:05.930 --> 00:12:08.810
statements of support and acknowledgement

00:12:10.994 --> 00:12:12.590
that this time is different,

00:12:12.590 --> 00:12:16.120
and our voices must be heard.

00:12:16.120 --> 00:12:19.610
And that is a bare minimum.

00:12:19.610 --> 00:12:24.610
You look at every professional
sports organization

00:12:24.760 --> 00:12:25.593
in this country.

00:12:25.593 --> 00:12:29.060
I think 31 or 32

00:12:29.060 --> 00:12:31.330
NFL football teams have
put out statements,

00:12:31.330 --> 00:12:32.480
and so forth and so on.

00:12:32.480 --> 00:12:33.850
So version 1.0

00:12:34.760 --> 00:12:37.313
largely revolves around words.

00:12:39.250 --> 00:12:42.430
Version 2.0 which is also underway

00:12:43.810 --> 00:12:47.160
involves checking the box

00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:50.540
around monetary donations,

00:12:50.540 --> 00:12:54.420
encouraging employees to give
to their favorite charity

00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:56.610
that could addresses some of these issues

00:12:56.610 --> 00:12:59.900
with companies matching those donations.

00:12:59.900 --> 00:13:02.750
Version 3.0 involves

00:13:02.750 --> 00:13:06.560
really substantial
financial contributions.

00:13:06.560 --> 00:13:10.530
Morgan Stanley, where I
worked for a long time,

00:13:10.530 --> 00:13:14.500
sent a $5 million check over
to the Legal Defense Fund,

00:13:14.500 --> 00:13:16.787
Thurgood's organization, last week,

00:13:16.787 --> 00:13:19.740
and we're gonna see a lot more of that.

00:13:19.740 --> 00:13:22.270
Bank of America announced
that it's gonna spend

00:13:22.270 --> 00:13:26.180
a billion dollars on doing this.

00:13:26.180 --> 00:13:30.280
But ultimately version
4.0 is gonna involve

00:13:30.280 --> 00:13:33.780
more than simply writing checks,

00:13:33.780 --> 00:13:36.950
and more than speaking words.

00:13:36.950 --> 00:13:40.200
It's gonna involve taking substantive

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:45.200
transformative action around change,

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:50.480
and it's gonna have to be
something that can be measured.

00:13:50.800 --> 00:13:54.300
I will make a small wager to anybody

00:13:54.300 --> 00:13:57.293
that in one year's time,

00:13:58.190 --> 00:14:02.870
every media outlet,
every television station,

00:14:02.870 --> 00:14:05.810
streaming service, every newspaper,

00:14:05.810 --> 00:14:07.770
will have a highly promoted

00:14:09.850 --> 00:14:12.380
feature program,

00:14:12.380 --> 00:14:16.900
and it'll be some version
of the following title.

00:14:16.900 --> 00:14:20.990
Memorial Day 2021, the
one-year anniversary

00:14:20.990 --> 00:14:25.113
of the death, the murder of
George Floyd, what has changed.

00:14:26.070 --> 00:14:29.450
And I can guarantee you that
everybody will look back

00:14:29.450 --> 00:14:30.930
and ask what has changed,

00:14:30.930 --> 00:14:33.840
and I think forward-thinking leaders

00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:37.460
will start thinking about that now.

00:14:37.460 --> 00:14:39.260
The one thing that the business community

00:14:39.260 --> 00:14:41.110
does extremely well,

00:14:41.110 --> 00:14:44.470
probably better than any other
institution in our country,

00:14:44.470 --> 00:14:48.123
and that is we measure
stuff; we measure everything.

00:14:49.160 --> 00:14:52.010
If it's important to us, we measure it.

00:14:52.010 --> 00:14:54.650
If it's important to us,
we hold people accountable.

00:14:54.650 --> 00:14:58.010
If people sort of accomplish their goals

00:14:58.010 --> 00:15:01.970
and do what we have set up to measure,

00:15:01.970 --> 00:15:03.120
then they get rewarded.

00:15:03.120 --> 00:15:06.210
They get promoted; they get
paid; they get rewarded.

00:15:06.210 --> 00:15:09.400
If they don't, then
they're dealt with as well.

00:15:09.400 --> 00:15:12.070
They get fired, they get
demoted, or what have you.

00:15:12.070 --> 00:15:17.070
And so the business
community is already having

00:15:17.540 --> 00:15:19.770
some soul-searching discussions

00:15:19.770 --> 00:15:22.120
around what are we gonna measure,

00:15:22.120 --> 00:15:24.730
and how are we gonna look
different a year from now?

00:15:24.730 --> 00:15:26.760
What will be our contribution

00:15:26.760 --> 00:15:30.430
to continuing the dialogs
that have started?

00:15:30.430 --> 00:15:32.090
I should have said also that

00:15:34.340 --> 00:15:36.320
even before version 1.0,

00:15:36.320 --> 00:15:39.390
the most basic thing that has to happen

00:15:39.390 --> 00:15:43.670
before we can go anywhere
will be honest conversations

00:15:43.670 --> 00:15:45.520
and getting out of your comfort zone.

00:15:45.520 --> 00:15:48.730
I have told all my partners at Lazard,

00:15:48.730 --> 00:15:51.130
there are only three African
American partners at Lazard,

00:15:51.130 --> 00:15:54.790
the rest are white; I've
told every single one of them

00:15:54.790 --> 00:15:59.000
that they need to go
through their contacts

00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:01.890
and identify seven to 10

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:07.150
African Americans whom they know.

00:16:07.150 --> 00:16:09.040
They may know 'em well;
they may not know 'em well.

00:16:09.040 --> 00:16:10.820
They may have been high school classmates.

00:16:10.820 --> 00:16:14.160
They may see 'em occasionally
in church or somewhere,

00:16:14.160 --> 00:16:15.830
but they need to call them,

00:16:15.830 --> 00:16:19.680
and they just need to listen
and hear and try to understand

00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:22.370
what is going on in this moment in time.

00:16:22.370 --> 00:16:26.700
It's a very very uncomfortable
conversation to have.

00:16:26.700 --> 00:16:28.080
It's probably not uncomfortable

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:29.780
for the first two or three people you call

00:16:29.780 --> 00:16:31.850
because you've known them for a long time,

00:16:31.850 --> 00:16:34.480
but to try to reach out to somebody

00:16:34.480 --> 00:16:37.520
that you haven talked
to in 10 or 15 years,

00:16:37.520 --> 00:16:41.070
and say, "Tell me what you're
experiencing right now"

00:16:41.070 --> 00:16:42.920
is something that I think should be

00:16:42.920 --> 00:16:47.590
a required moral exercise for
everybody in this country.

00:16:47.590 --> 00:16:50.130
And by the way, I'm in turn reaching out

00:16:50.130 --> 00:16:53.380
to the hundreds and hundreds of people

00:16:53.380 --> 00:16:56.677
that I have interacted with,
the thousands of people

00:16:56.677 --> 00:17:00.857
that I have interacted with
over my lifetime to say,

00:17:00.857 --> 00:17:03.487
"I'm calling you because I know

00:17:03.487 --> 00:17:05.117
"you're thinking about these issues,

00:17:05.117 --> 00:17:08.167
"and I know you're
wondering what can you do."

00:17:10.870 --> 00:17:14.070
- And it's super helpful
if we imagine ourselves

00:17:14.070 --> 00:17:15.680
a year in the future, and you know,

00:17:15.680 --> 00:17:17.230
we're having this conversation again.

00:17:17.230 --> 00:17:18.840
I agree with you, there
probably will be shows

00:17:18.840 --> 00:17:19.673
to that nature.

00:17:19.673 --> 00:17:23.640
We're getting questions
off of Facebook and YouTube

00:17:23.640 --> 00:17:25.890
on, you know, what are the levers

00:17:25.890 --> 00:17:27.990
that hopefully will change by then.

00:17:27.990 --> 00:17:30.507
You know, Maya Balaraman says,

00:17:30.507 --> 00:17:32.257
"What are your thoughts
on people reacting to this

00:17:32.257 --> 00:17:35.360
"in the other extreme, defunding police?"

00:17:35.360 --> 00:17:37.180
Actually, Susana Garcia
Dominguez is asking that,

00:17:37.180 --> 00:17:38.360
and obviously you have a relationship

00:17:38.360 --> 00:17:41.883
with some of the police
groups inside of New York.

00:17:42.950 --> 00:17:44.387
Obviously, emotions are
running very high right now,

00:17:44.387 --> 00:17:49.387
but what are the levers that
you would like to see changed

00:17:49.600 --> 00:17:52.550
a year from now or two years
from now or 10 years from now?

00:17:55.610 --> 00:17:59.500
- Well, first of all,
let's just talk a minute

00:17:59.500 --> 00:18:02.470
about defunding the police.

00:18:02.470 --> 00:18:06.973
I think unfortunately, excuse
me, that's a charged word.

00:18:08.660 --> 00:18:11.630
I think people need to appreciate

00:18:11.630 --> 00:18:13.883
that by default,

00:18:14.720 --> 00:18:16.300
our police departments have been asked

00:18:16.300 --> 00:18:18.370
to do a lot of things.

00:18:18.370 --> 00:18:22.120
And fundamentally, our police are here

00:18:22.120 --> 00:18:24.413
to protect and to serve us,

00:18:25.430 --> 00:18:27.630
but our police are not social workers,

00:18:27.630 --> 00:18:30.273
and our police are not healthcare workers.

00:18:32.010 --> 00:18:34.830
Early in New York City,
we asked our police

00:18:34.830 --> 00:18:37.023
to enforce social distancing.

00:18:39.367 --> 00:18:42.660
And that's a charged word, enforce.

00:18:42.660 --> 00:18:47.660
Police aren't trained to
enforce social distancing.

00:18:47.730 --> 00:18:52.150
And so if you look at the budgets

00:18:52.150 --> 00:18:54.000
of the police department,

00:18:54.000 --> 00:18:55.610
and you look at some of the things

00:18:55.610 --> 00:18:56.660
that police have been asked to do,

00:18:56.660 --> 00:18:59.260
so for instance, a lot was made

00:18:59.260 --> 00:19:01.810
of us shutting down our subway system

00:19:01.810 --> 00:19:05.130
for the first time in a hundred plus years

00:19:05.130 --> 00:19:08.080
in order to clean it every night

00:19:08.080 --> 00:19:09.260
between the hours of one o'clock

00:19:09.260 --> 00:19:11.390
and five 'clock in the morning.

00:19:11.390 --> 00:19:14.570
Police were asked to go into the subway

00:19:16.460 --> 00:19:19.310
to assist the homeless
and to remove the homeless

00:19:19.310 --> 00:19:21.393
in order to disinfect those trains.

00:19:22.550 --> 00:19:24.560
It's questionable as to whether or not

00:19:24.560 --> 00:19:27.820
that's a job for the police.

00:19:27.820 --> 00:19:31.200
What the police decided
to do was to take nurses

00:19:31.200 --> 00:19:35.600
into the subway stations with them,

00:19:35.600 --> 00:19:39.500
and homeless advocates with them

00:19:39.500 --> 00:19:42.210
to figure out what to do

00:19:42.210 --> 00:19:44.660
with the homeless people
in the subway stations.

00:19:44.660 --> 00:19:49.020
That is money that the
police department is spending

00:19:49.020 --> 00:19:53.177
on services that
fundamentally aren't policing.

00:19:53.177 --> 00:19:56.010
And so when we say defunding,

00:19:56.010 --> 00:19:58.420
what we're really talking
about, what I think people

00:19:58.420 --> 00:20:01.600
are talking about is reallocating money

00:20:01.600 --> 00:20:05.190
that has been parked with
the police department

00:20:05.190 --> 00:20:08.400
to cover a whole range of services

00:20:08.400 --> 00:20:11.740
that aren't policing in their orientation.

00:20:11.740 --> 00:20:14.020
By the way, as an analogy as well,

00:20:14.020 --> 00:20:16.160
the largest part of the
New York City budget

00:20:16.160 --> 00:20:17.870
is on education.

00:20:17.870 --> 00:20:20.050
We spend $30 billion

00:20:20.050 --> 00:20:24.650
of our $90 billion budget on education.

00:20:24.650 --> 00:20:28.750
But we do a lot more in our
public schools than educate.

00:20:28.750 --> 00:20:31.390
We serve more meals in the
New York City public schools

00:20:31.390 --> 00:20:36.350
than any other institution
other than the U.S. military.

00:20:36.350 --> 00:20:40.613
So we are a huge provider of food.

00:20:41.470 --> 00:20:43.710
We provide, in effect, shelter.

00:20:43.710 --> 00:20:45.103
We provide daycare,

00:20:46.454 --> 00:20:48.780
and so one could argue

00:20:48.780 --> 00:20:53.780
that those funds should really
be under health and welfare,

00:20:54.900 --> 00:20:56.690
housing or what have you.

00:20:56.690 --> 00:21:00.560
So I don't think
defunding in and of itself

00:21:00.560 --> 00:21:04.640
of the police department
or the education department

00:21:04.640 --> 00:21:06.990
is what we're talking about.

00:21:06.990 --> 00:21:09.580
We're really talking
about allowing the police

00:21:09.580 --> 00:21:11.320
to do what they're supposed to do

00:21:12.820 --> 00:21:15.500
in a smart and intelligent
and thoughtful way,

00:21:15.500 --> 00:21:16.640
in a respectful way.

00:21:16.640 --> 00:21:18.223
Now having said that,

00:21:19.790 --> 00:21:22.910
there are very very very specific

00:21:24.460 --> 00:21:28.120
things that should be addressed

00:21:28.120 --> 00:21:31.460
as they relate to policing reform.

00:21:31.460 --> 00:21:35.010
The current head of the Legal Defense Fund

00:21:36.211 --> 00:21:39.980
is a woman named Sherrilyn
Ifill, I-f-I-l-l.

00:21:39.980 --> 00:21:44.270
She just wrote a very detailed article

00:21:44.270 --> 00:21:45.900
in "Slate" magazine.

00:21:45.900 --> 00:21:47.800
It's about three days' old.

00:21:47.800 --> 00:21:50.570
She lists probably 15

00:21:50.570 --> 00:21:54.170
very specific and reasonable reforms

00:21:54.170 --> 00:21:56.440
for our nation's police department.

00:21:56.440 --> 00:22:01.040
I would encourage everybody
to read those reforms.

00:22:01.040 --> 00:22:03.230
They've very sensible.

00:22:03.230 --> 00:22:06.230
They are in the best
interests of our country.

00:22:06.230 --> 00:22:10.050
If you ask what should
change within the next year,

00:22:10.050 --> 00:22:14.035
I would start there because
those are actionable items,

00:22:14.035 --> 00:22:18.240
and acting on those items will tell us

00:22:18.240 --> 00:22:19.973
what side are you on.

00:22:21.208 --> 00:22:24.870
And not acting on those
items similarly will tell us

00:22:24.870 --> 00:22:27.193
what side are you on.

00:22:30.110 --> 00:22:32.570
- That's super valuable, and you know,

00:22:33.560 --> 00:22:34.393
is the nature of that,

00:22:34.393 --> 00:22:36.430
I'm definitely gonna take a
look at that right after this,

00:22:36.430 --> 00:22:39.780
is it the nature of it is
things like improving training?

00:22:39.780 --> 00:22:43.920
Is it things like maybe be
it'd easier for a sheriff

00:22:43.920 --> 00:22:46.700
to let someone go who's
under disciplinary action,

00:22:46.700 --> 00:22:48.060
things like that?

00:22:48.060 --> 00:22:52.010
- You start with the most
sensible and the most basic.

00:22:52.010 --> 00:22:55.200
There should be a database,
a national database

00:22:57.550 --> 00:23:00.720
on all law enforcement officials.

00:23:00.720 --> 00:23:05.720
If you get fired for
killing somebody illegally

00:23:06.300 --> 00:23:09.410
in New York City by using a chokehold,

00:23:09.410 --> 00:23:11.470
you shouldn't be able
to go across the river

00:23:11.470 --> 00:23:14.383
and get rehired in Newark, New Jersey.

00:23:15.260 --> 00:23:18.700
Right now, there's no way for us to track

00:23:19.890 --> 00:23:22.960
those kind of offenses
on a nationwide basis.

00:23:22.960 --> 00:23:24.860
That's a huge loophole

00:23:26.370 --> 00:23:28.290
in the enforcement of civil rights

00:23:28.290 --> 00:23:30.143
and dealing with injustices.

00:23:30.980 --> 00:23:33.420
We have something in New York

00:23:33.420 --> 00:23:37.840
that's called Section 50A.

00:23:37.840 --> 00:23:39.280
It revolves around

00:23:40.700 --> 00:23:43.780
disclosing certain information,

00:23:43.780 --> 00:23:46.120
certain disciplinary
information on all cops.

00:23:46.120 --> 00:23:47.740
Right now, that is private information.

00:23:47.740 --> 00:23:50.863
Many cities make that information public.

00:23:51.890 --> 00:23:53.810
We are talking about training.

00:23:53.810 --> 00:23:56.040
We are talking about deescalation.

00:23:56.040 --> 00:24:01.040
I like to say that every cop
should wake up every morning,

00:24:01.110 --> 00:24:03.760
and before he or she brushes his teeth

00:24:03.760 --> 00:24:07.220
should repeat the word
deescalation 10 times.

00:24:07.220 --> 00:24:09.820
That should be the mantra.

00:24:09.820 --> 00:24:13.140
The vast vast majority of encounters

00:24:13.140 --> 00:24:16.660
between the police and our citizens

00:24:17.600 --> 00:24:21.310
should result in some
sort of deescalation.

00:24:21.310 --> 00:24:25.400
And New York City spends a lot of time

00:24:25.400 --> 00:24:29.210
training its officers
on how to deescalate.

00:24:29.210 --> 00:24:32.890
And by the way,
deescalation is much easier

00:24:32.890 --> 00:24:37.460
when the police officer looks
very much like the community

00:24:37.460 --> 00:24:39.238
in which he's policing.

00:24:39.238 --> 00:24:44.238
And so it looks very different
when the police officer

00:24:44.350 --> 00:24:47.750
does not look like the community
in which he is policing.

00:24:47.750 --> 00:24:51.403
Police officers are meant to
be guardians, not warriors.

00:24:53.229 --> 00:24:56.387
And that is sort of a
critical part of our training.

00:24:56.387 --> 00:24:58.980
And I don't have the list in front of me,

00:24:58.980 --> 00:25:00.410
and I haven't committed it to memory

00:25:00.410 --> 00:25:01.705
which I probably should,

00:25:01.705 --> 00:25:05.250
but I would just advise
people to just pull it up.

00:25:05.250 --> 00:25:07.000
You could easily pull it up online.

00:25:08.080 --> 00:25:09.390
- That's super helpful,

00:25:09.390 --> 00:25:12.340
and we have some questions here,

00:25:12.340 --> 00:25:15.200
where Maximilien Bissonnette,

00:25:15.200 --> 00:25:17.110
about, well, they're asking for a question

00:25:17.110 --> 00:25:18.820
around systemic racism,

00:25:18.820 --> 00:25:20.890
a lesson on systemic
racism on Khan Academy.

00:25:20.890 --> 00:25:23.090
But this term systemic racism

00:25:23.090 --> 00:25:25.513
is something we're hearing a lot of now.

00:25:27.198 --> 00:25:29.450
How do you think that
manifests itself in this?

00:25:29.450 --> 00:25:31.570
And it's obviously broader
than this notion of policing,

00:25:31.570 --> 00:25:33.830
'cause a lot of what
you just described are

00:25:33.830 --> 00:25:35.830
kind of it seems very pragmatic policies

00:25:35.830 --> 00:25:37.300
to have better accountability,

00:25:37.300 --> 00:25:41.500
better transparency, better
training for police officers.

00:25:41.500 --> 00:25:43.750
You know, the whole deescalation
thing makes a lot of sense,

00:25:43.750 --> 00:25:47.201
but that still doesn't seem to
necessarily get at the heart

00:25:47.201 --> 00:25:50.610
of potentially, you know, systemic racism,

00:25:50.610 --> 00:25:53.657
and how systemic racism,

00:25:53.657 --> 00:25:55.250
and it depends on how you define it,

00:25:55.250 --> 00:25:57.180
it pervades more of society.

00:25:57.180 --> 00:26:00.130
What does that term mean to
you and how do you get at that?

00:26:01.500 --> 00:26:03.280
- What we saw

00:26:03.280 --> 00:26:08.200
in the case of the Central Park birder

00:26:08.200 --> 00:26:10.493
was systemic racism.

00:26:13.420 --> 00:26:17.000
I am sure that that 40-year-old graduate

00:26:17.000 --> 00:26:18.690
of the University of Chicago

00:26:20.550 --> 00:26:24.040
living in one of the
most liberal communities

00:26:24.040 --> 00:26:28.230
in the United States, the
Upper West Side of Manhattan,

00:26:28.230 --> 00:26:33.230
working at one of the highest profiled

00:26:34.500 --> 00:26:37.790
financial services company in the world

00:26:39.390 --> 00:26:41.660
exposed on a regular basis

00:26:41.660 --> 00:26:46.513
to a very very diverse
community and population,

00:26:47.990 --> 00:26:52.930
very very thoughtfully
announced in advance

00:26:52.930 --> 00:26:56.930
how she was gonna lie
to the police department

00:26:56.930 --> 00:27:01.930
about what was unfolding in
that park in broad daylight.

00:27:02.960 --> 00:27:03.963
By the way,

00:27:07.160 --> 00:27:08.820
that could've resulted in a death

00:27:09.758 --> 00:27:12.220
of that African American man

00:27:12.220 --> 00:27:13.960
because if the police had shown up

00:27:13.960 --> 00:27:16.623
and it was sort of his
word against her word,

00:27:17.910 --> 00:27:19.610
and if he had done anything,

00:27:19.610 --> 00:27:21.900
moved his hand very
quickly or what have you,

00:27:21.900 --> 00:27:23.370
he could've been dead.

00:27:23.370 --> 00:27:28.213
That is the raw definition
of systemic racism.

00:27:29.723 --> 00:27:31.310
And Sal, I don't know

00:27:32.700 --> 00:27:34.370
how we deal with that

00:27:35.950 --> 00:27:37.680
until we have had

00:27:38.620 --> 00:27:41.350
an ongoing and sustained

00:27:41.350 --> 00:27:45.573
national conversation around race.

00:27:46.840 --> 00:27:50.840
And I know the people like Bryan Stevenson

00:27:50.840 --> 00:27:52.270
at the Equal Justice Institute

00:27:52.270 --> 00:27:54.980
and Darren Walker at the Ford Foundation

00:27:54.980 --> 00:27:58.010
are trying to facilitate that.

00:27:58.010 --> 00:28:00.600
But in this moment in time,

00:28:00.600 --> 00:28:02.770
I think we are talking

00:28:02.770 --> 00:28:06.010
about a series,

00:28:06.010 --> 00:28:10.680
an ongoing series of discussions

00:28:12.910 --> 00:28:15.240
around racism

00:28:16.150 --> 00:28:19.100
and how did we get here.

00:28:19.100 --> 00:28:21.330
Coming back to what you do so well,

00:28:21.330 --> 00:28:22.683
and that is education,

00:28:24.520 --> 00:28:27.860
I think there is a huge opportunity,

00:28:27.860 --> 00:28:31.000
a huge, a must-do,

00:28:31.000 --> 00:28:36.000
for us to think about
how we put this topic

00:28:36.320 --> 00:28:40.240
into our classrooms early.

00:28:40.240 --> 00:28:44.260
How do we instruct our teachers

00:28:44.260 --> 00:28:46.160
to have this discussion

00:28:46.160 --> 00:28:51.160
with four-year-olds and
five-year-olds and six-year-olds?

00:28:51.370 --> 00:28:55.860
We are amazingly creative and thoughtful

00:28:55.860 --> 00:28:59.520
about using technology
and the like to solve

00:28:59.520 --> 00:29:04.040
some really really really
tough education questions.

00:29:04.040 --> 00:29:06.050
I think that we're gonna have to think

00:29:06.050 --> 00:29:11.050
really really differently about
how we get the discussion,

00:29:11.490 --> 00:29:13.250
and how we get exercises

00:29:13.250 --> 00:29:14.630
and how we teach

00:29:15.990 --> 00:29:20.453
the whole concept of systemic racism.

00:29:21.400 --> 00:29:23.190
I don't know.

00:29:23.190 --> 00:29:25.967
I don't have an answer for that,

00:29:25.967 --> 00:29:28.260
but I would say

00:29:28.260 --> 00:29:32.900
that I believe the vast
majority of parents

00:29:34.080 --> 00:29:37.300
were shocked when they heard the videotape

00:29:37.300 --> 00:29:39.565
of the woman in Central Park,

00:29:39.565 --> 00:29:43.100
and I believe that the
vast majority of parents

00:29:43.100 --> 00:29:44.170
would be appalled

00:29:45.301 --> 00:29:47.920
if they knew that they had friends

00:29:47.920 --> 00:29:48.960
who could talk like that,

00:29:48.960 --> 00:29:51.530
or God forbid, their children
could talk like this.

00:29:51.530 --> 00:29:54.610
And so I pose this as a real opportunity

00:29:54.610 --> 00:29:58.093
and a real challenge for our educators.

00:30:06.210 --> 00:30:08.717
Can't hear, I can't hear you (laughs).

00:30:10.270 --> 00:30:12.320
- Sorry, no, my bad, my kids are making

00:30:12.320 --> 00:30:15.050
a ridiculous amount of noise downstairs.

00:30:15.050 --> 00:30:17.480
They're just playing
the piano and screaming.

00:30:17.480 --> 00:30:19.110
Anyway, this is

00:30:19.110 --> 00:30:19.943
- Call the police (laughs)!
- what you get

00:30:19.943 --> 00:30:21.100
working from home (laughs).

00:30:24.740 --> 00:30:25.690
I've threatened it.

00:30:30.160 --> 00:30:31.880
My five-year-old is under the impression

00:30:31.880 --> 00:30:33.367
that the police are,

00:30:33.367 --> 00:30:34.880
and this goes maybe to
your previous point,

00:30:34.880 --> 00:30:37.360
maybe the police are invoked
a little bit too much,

00:30:37.360 --> 00:30:39.610
but I've definitely
threatened him sometimes.

00:30:41.980 --> 00:30:44.170
I guess maybe some final words,

00:30:44.170 --> 00:30:45.360
there's obviously a lot of people

00:30:45.360 --> 00:30:48.993
with a lot of energy right now around it.

00:30:51.050 --> 00:30:54.720
I like this idea of the
very tangible things

00:30:54.720 --> 00:30:55.740
mentioned in the "Slate" article,

00:30:55.740 --> 00:30:57.930
and I think our team has just
put the link to that article

00:30:57.930 --> 00:30:59.850
in the message boards
on Facebook and YouTube

00:30:59.850 --> 00:31:01.200
for everyone to look at it,

00:31:02.050 --> 00:31:05.050
but what should happen to the
energy around the protests?

00:31:05.050 --> 00:31:06.147
There is a world where they say,

00:31:06.147 --> 00:31:09.610
"Hey, here are the five
reforms we need to see."

00:31:09.610 --> 00:31:10.470
There's a world where they're saying

00:31:10.470 --> 00:31:13.040
we're just gonna keep doing it
'cause people need to see it.

00:31:13.040 --> 00:31:14.820
You know, there might be
elements in the protests

00:31:14.820 --> 00:31:16.540
of even also economic

00:31:18.240 --> 00:31:20.060
anger or displacement

00:31:20.060 --> 00:31:25.060
that are mixed in with
the systemic racism.

00:31:25.520 --> 00:31:27.090
How do you think the
protests are gonna evolve,

00:31:27.090 --> 00:31:27.923
and what advice would you have

00:31:27.923 --> 00:31:30.360
for the people who are on the streets?

00:31:30.360 --> 00:31:31.510
How should that evolve?

00:31:36.960 --> 00:31:38.770
- Excuse me, we had

00:31:40.740 --> 00:31:45.190
massive protests, civil
unrest in the early '60s,

00:31:45.190 --> 00:31:49.360
and that led to the Voting Rights Act

00:31:49.360 --> 00:31:53.633
and the Civil Rights Act,
one in 1964 and one in 1965.

00:31:58.400 --> 00:32:02.970
The immediate result of these protests

00:32:03.890 --> 00:32:07.830
should lead to people coming
out to vote in November

00:32:07.830 --> 00:32:10.963
and making changes from top to the bottom.

00:32:16.210 --> 00:32:19.880
People chose not to vote in 2016

00:32:22.140 --> 00:32:24.290
for any number of reasons,

00:32:24.290 --> 00:32:29.290
and there were consequences to not voting.

00:32:29.310 --> 00:32:32.930
The most basic right that we all have

00:32:32.930 --> 00:32:36.800
and the most basic
obligation that we all have

00:32:36.800 --> 00:32:40.410
is to translate protest into action,

00:32:40.410 --> 00:32:43.460
and the most basic action that we can take

00:32:43.460 --> 00:32:46.080
revolves around voting,

00:32:46.080 --> 00:32:50.900
and voting at the federal level,
voting at the state level,

00:32:50.900 --> 00:32:53.720
and voting at the local level.

00:32:53.720 --> 00:32:56.960
The voices are there unlike any

00:32:56.960 --> 00:33:00.083
that most of the people on
this call have every seen.

00:33:01.410 --> 00:33:02.913
And the will is there.

00:33:03.830 --> 00:33:08.757
And the vast vast vast
majority of the protesters

00:33:09.600 --> 00:33:11.950
in excess of probably 99%,

00:33:11.950 --> 00:33:14.390
are protesting peacefully,

00:33:14.390 --> 00:33:18.220
and they need to continue
this peaceful protest

00:33:19.160 --> 00:33:24.100
right up through election day in November.

00:33:24.100 --> 00:33:28.490
The other thing that
people around the country

00:33:28.490 --> 00:33:30.980
should be doing is

00:33:31.930 --> 00:33:35.470
going to visit en masse

00:33:35.470 --> 00:33:38.700
their police chief and their mayors

00:33:38.700 --> 00:33:42.960
and demanding an answer
to the following question:

00:33:42.960 --> 00:33:47.560
how can we be assured that
what happened in Minneapolis

00:33:47.560 --> 00:33:51.460
will never happen in our city?

00:33:51.460 --> 00:33:53.290
It could be a city of 10,000 people.

00:33:53.290 --> 00:33:55.740
It could be a city of 100,000 people,

00:33:55.740 --> 00:33:59.700
but people need to get into
the nitty gritty and understand

00:34:00.890 --> 00:34:03.820
if what they saw happening in Minneapolis

00:34:03.820 --> 00:34:07.480
could happen in their hometown.

00:34:07.480 --> 00:34:10.720
But I think, Sal, the most important thing

00:34:11.940 --> 00:34:15.150
that everybody can do

00:34:15.150 --> 00:34:19.960
is translate protest into voting.

00:34:19.960 --> 00:34:23.853
The business community will
be doing a lot of things.

00:34:24.900 --> 00:34:26.880
And I'm sure the not-for-profit world

00:34:26.880 --> 00:34:28.510
will be doing a lot of things,

00:34:28.510 --> 00:34:31.600
and the education community
will be doing a lot of things.

00:34:31.600 --> 00:34:33.760
They will all be very very different.

00:34:33.760 --> 00:34:38.760
But the one thing that we
can all do which is identical

00:34:39.120 --> 00:34:42.150
and it is a requirement, it is a right,

00:34:42.150 --> 00:34:45.263
it is an obligation, is to vote.

00:34:48.970 --> 00:34:53.050
- Yeah, no, I think those are
very actionable doable things

00:34:53.050 --> 00:34:56.510
for anyone listening.

